❓Question
Given an integer array nums and an integer target, return all unique quadruplets [nums[a], nums[b], nums[c], nums[d]] such that:
0 <= a, b, c, d < n
a, b, c, d are distinct
nums[a] + nums[b] + nums[c] + nums[d] == target
The answer can be returned in any order.

Example
Input:
nums = [1,0,-1,0,-2,2], target = 0
Output:
[[-2,-1,1,2],
 [-2,0,0,2],
 [-1,0,0,1]]

✅Approach — Sorting + Two Pointers
First sort the array.
Use a to select the first number.
Use b to select the second number.
Use two pointers:
c = b + 1
d = nums.length - 1
Calculate the sum of the four numbers.
If:
sum == target → store quadruplet and move both pointers.
sum < target → c++
sum > target → d--
Skip duplicate values for a, b, c, and d to ensure unique quadruplets.

Code
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> fourSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> list = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        for (int a = 0; a < nums.length; a++) {
            if (a > 0 && nums[a] == nums[a - 1]) {
                continue;
            }
            for (int b = a + 1; b < nums.length; b++) {
                if (b > a + 1 && nums[b] == nums[b - 1]) {
                    continue;
                }
                int c = b + 1;
                int d = nums.length - 1;
                while (c < d) {
                    long sum = (long) nums[a]
                             + nums[b]
                             + nums[c]
                             + nums[d];
                    if (sum == target) {
                        list.add(Arrays.asList(
                            nums[a], nums[b], nums[c], nums[d]
                        ));

                        while (c < d && nums[c] == nums[c + 1]) {
                            c++;
                        }
                        while (c < d && nums[d] == nums[d - 1]) {
                            d--;
                        }
                        c++;
                        d--;

                    } else if (sum < target) {
                        c++;
                    } else {
                        d--;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return list;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n³)
Sorting = O(n log n)
Three nested levels effectively give O(n³)
Space Complexity: O(1)
